\begin{abstract}
The intensification of disturbances caused by climate change, exemplified by Storm Vaia in 2018, has significant impacts on Alpine forest ecosystems. Since traditional binary classifications fail to capture the complexity of ecological transitions, this thesis proposes a new framework based on Fuzzy logic and Ridgeline graphs, applied to Sentinel-2 time series (2017-2025). Moving beyond the rigid “forest/non-forest” dichotomy, the study analyses recovery trajectories in six affected areas, modelling regeneration as a continuous gradient. The results reveal a distinct phenomenon of early greening, driven by pioneer vegetation and accelerated by salvage logging, which often masks incomplete structural restoration. The approach presented proves to be a valid alternative to conventional techniques, providing managers with a tool capable of decoupling simple vegetative vigour from functional recovery dynamics.
\end{abstract}
