\section{Introduction} \label{secInt}
Forest ecosystems are currently facing unprecedented challenges driven by global climate change. In recent decades, disturbances affecting mountain forests have occurred with greater frequency and caused increasingly significant damage, but they are expected to happen more in the future. The most common disturbance agents are windthrows and bark beetles \cite{Piazza2024}. These are usually part of the dynamics in ecosystems and play a role in renewing the forest, creating heterogeneity and promoting biodiversity \cite{Seidl2017}. 

Historically, forest management in the European Alps has been heavily oriented towards maximizing timber production, a priority that has profoundly shaped the structural and specific composition of contemporary woodlands. \begin{sloppypar} Over the last two centuries, silvicultural practices have favored the widespread establishment of monospecific, even-aged spruce (\textit{Picea abies}) stands, often extending their range well beyond their optimal ecological niche \cite{Motta2018}. \end{sloppypar}
While these simplified forest structures ensured high economic yields and efficient harvesting operations, they progressively reduced the natural heterogeneity of the Alpine landscape. By homogenizing age classes and reducing species diversity, this management approach inadvertently eroded the ecological resilience of these ecosystems, making them highly susceptible to abiotic stressors. This structural simplification has rendered Alpine forests increasingly vulnerable to the shifting disturbance regimes driven by global climate change. 

In this critical scenario, the Vaia storm, which hit Northeastern Italy in late October 2018, represents a perfect example of an extreme event. Characterised by wind gusts exceeding 200 km/h, the storm caused massive destruction across the Alpine region, damaging approximately 42,500 hectares of forest and felling over 8.5 million cubic meters of timber \cite{Chirici2019}. The magnitude of Vaia exposed the intrinsic fragility of these uniform, even-aged forest structures to wind disturbances, confirming the risks associated with simplified silvicultural models \cite{Motta2018}. 

The main objective of the thesis is to monitor and quantify the ecological recovery of forests affected by Storm Vaia (2018) in north-eastern Italy. The study aims to overcome the limitations of classic binary analyses (forest/non-forest) to assess forest restoration as a continuous gradient.
This approach permits the evaluation of complex spatial dynamics, including the early greening driven by pioneer vegetation and to provide useful tools for forest management.
\section{Literature review}
\subsection{The Vaia storm} 

The weather event known as ‘Storm Vaia’, which developed between 27 and 30 October 2018, was characterised by a deep depression that generated gusts of southerly wind with peaks exceeding 200 km/h \cite{Chirici2019}. These winds caused severe damage to forests in Lombardy, Veneto, Trentino-Alto Adige and Friuli Venezia-Giulia \cite{Motta2018}. 

This intensity caused unprecedented damage in the Eastern Alps, mainly affecting spruce and spruce-fir forests \cite{Chirici2019}. The damage, as indicated in the work of \hyperref[bib:Motta2018]{Motta et al. (2018)}, affected various forest categories, characterised by multiple structural types and altitude differences. Forest stability is determined by critical wind speed thresholds; beyond these limits, mechanical failure occurs. The thresholds of mechanical resistance to breaking vary considerably: while a single tree breaks at around 94-100 km/h, for more resistant forest formations this limit can rise to 150 km/h \cite{Motta2018}.  In the case of Vaia, these thresholds were exceeded in several areas. 

Overall, the storm knocked down approximately 8.5 million m³ of timber across an area of over 42,500 hectares. Although regionally the damage affected about 3\% of the forest area, locally the destruction of the forest reached peaks of 47\%, radically altering the ecosystem continuity of entire forest districts \cite{Chirici2019}. 

Vaia not only had an immediate ecological impact, but also a socio-economic one. Post-event management involved salvage logging in many cases, with the intervention executed using hervesting systems (highly mechanized systems, cable-yarding systems, skidder and tractor systems) ranging from a total removal of damaged trees or a partial one. This practice was carried out to maximise revenue before the wood on the ground was damaged and to reduce the proliferation of the European spruce bark beetle (\textit{Ips typographus}), which finds dead wood to be the ideal place for outbreaks. However, these operations caused a second anthropogenic disturbance that transformed the forests into veritable construction sites. The use of heavy machinery to remove fallen logs causes a loss of residual vegetation and an alteration of the soil structure, which modifies the initial conditions for vegetation recolonisation \cite{Marangon2024}.  

\subsection{Ecology of disturbances and forest continuity}  

When we talk about disturbance ecology, we must first understand what is meant by disturbance. Disturbance, in fact, represents “the set of mechanisms and processes that alter the balance of one or more levels of the ecological hierarchy, from the individual to the ecosystem” \cite{Posceddu2020}.  

While Chapter 2.1 illustrated the specific magnitude and consequences of the Vaia storm, this section establishes the theoretical framework. Building upon the general definition, the analysis now shifts to the mechanisms of post-disturbance recovery, particularly starting from biological legacies.

“In an ecological context, we define “biological legacies” as the organisms, organic materials, and organically-generated environmental patterns that persist through a disturbance and are incorporated into the recovering ecosystem. Organisms may persist as intact organisms or as seed banks, spores, fungal hyphae, and parts (such as rhizomes) with a capacity to regenerate the whole organism” \cite{Franklin2000}.
This is a key concept for understanding post-Vaia resilience because, contrary to the perception of a destructive event creating a “tabula rasa”, \hyperref[bib:Franklin2000]{Franklin et al. (2000)} highlight how natural disturbances leave a considerable amount of living organisms and organic structures on the ground. 
 
These organisms leave a legacy and act as a link between the pre-disturbance and post-disturbance forest, accelerating recolonisation processes and maintaining ecosystem functions.   

The forest will now follow ecological succession, starting from what remains in the affected areas. In this regard, the work carried out by \hyperref[bib:Giupponi2023]{Giupponi et al. (2023)} is important, in which it is indicated that "the final stage of succession that occurs after the blowdown disturbance could be: (1) a forest similar to the pre-disturbance one, (2) a different forest (from a floristic, physiognomic and/or ecological point of view) compared to the pre-disturbance one, or (3) another type of vegetation (shrubland, grassland, etc.)". 

The disruption of the prevailing forest structure represents an alteration of microclimatic conditions, such as canopy cover, soil conditions, and the availability and distribution of resources and microhabitats \cite{Gazzea2025}. 

As observed by \hyperref[bib:Giupponi2023]{Giupponi et al. (2023)} in the areas affected by Vaia, there has been an explosive proliferation of herbaceous and shrubby species. This phenomenon has fundamental implications for the study of recovery in the context of interspecific competition. The work of \hyperref[bib:Piazza2024]{Piazza et al. (2024)} contributes by indicating that in the areas affected by Vaia, in the phases following the disturbance, there is an increase in the heterogeneity of microsites due to the greater availability of light and nutrients. These conditions favour the development of pioneer species, such as tall grasses and brambles, which can prevent or delay the establishment of seedlings germinated after the disturbance, while seedlings and saplings in advanced regeneration can benefit from accelerated growth thanks to full light conditions in areas disturbed by wind. 

\subsection{Ecosystem services and protection}

Disastrous events such as Vaia not only cause damage to forests but also have major repercussions on their ecosystem services. This chapter examines the primary services related to protection against natural hazards. This aspect concerns events such as rockfalls, avalanches and surface erosion that can have impacts on people leaving in these areas. The collapse of many trees, which in some areas corresponds to entire slopes near mountain villages, suggests that the vulnerability of these areas is increasing.   

 The study conducted by \hyperref[bib:Costa2021]{Costa et al. (2021)} seeks to understand how the interactions of natural disturbances interface with the multifunctionality of mountain forests. Specifically, they demonstrate how, in the immediate aftermath of the storm, through simulations of rockfalls along a slope affected by Vaia, the effectiveness of forest protection increases compared to the previous forest. This is because biological legacies (mainly dead wood on the ground) increase the roughness of the surface, creating a positive barrier effect.   

 Considering slope stability, with a primary focus on avoiding surface landslides, the presence of dead logs on the ground has a relevant impact. Root system of dead trees maintains a residual soil cohesion capacity for several years before degrading, while the removal of timber through salvage logging can accelerate erosion due to mechanical disturbance of the soil \cite{Lindenmayer2006}. 

 A fundamental concept in this case is the protection gap, which is defined as the period of time during which the protective function of the forest against natural hazards (avalanches, rockfalls) is compromised or insufficient. The work of \hyperref[bib:Costa2021]{Costa et al. (2021)} shows that this should be the main target in forest management strategies. 
 
In this context, maintaining wood on the ground not only protects human infrastructure but also helps to close this gap by protecting regeneration itself. As highlighted by \hyperref[bib:Piazza2024]{Piazza et al. (2024)}, structural complexity on the ground creates protected micro-sites that defend young seedlings from snow movement and ungulates, promoting the establishment of new forest. This reduces the protection gap, which is avoided until the dead wood degrades and the regeneration that has taken place at the same time is exposed. For this reason, it is suggested that the removal of logs on the ground increases the exposure of infrastructure to risks related to rockfalls because the protective effect is eliminated \cite{Costa2021}.

\subsection{Remote sensing for forest monitoring}

The vastness and fragmentation of the areas affected by Vaia, as well as the practical difficulties in reaching some areas due to the intricate structure of the forest, made field monitoring very complicated. In this context, the use of satellite imagery was of vital help.   

Among the various platforms available, the Sentinel-2 mission, which is part of the European Copernicus programme, played a key role. In fact, as described in the report by \hyperref[bib:Chirici2019]{Chirici et al. (2019)}, which provided the first official estimate of the damage caused by the storm, the use of Sentinel-2 data offers an optimal compromise thanks to three key features: spatial resolution (10-20 m), revisit frequency (5-10 days) and large-scale accessibility, as the data is provided through an open-access policy. Another key point is the possibility of using time series. In fact, the analysis of a continuous data flow makes it possible to go beyond the bi-temporal “before and after” comparison, perceiving phenological dynamics and changes in vegetation cover \cite{Giannetti2021}. 

A further perspective, as highlighted in the work of \hyperref[bib:Piragnolo2021]{Piragnolo et al. (2021)}, is represented by the integration of Sentinel-2 with cloud computing platforms (e.g. Google Earth Engine) and machine learning algorithms to map and monitor forest disturbances on a large scale with a high level of accuracy. 

Satellite imagery allows the use of spectral indices that provide interpretable ecological information. The most widely used vegetation index is the Normalised Difference Vegetation Index (NDVI), which is based on the contrast between the reflectance of vegetation in red, which is absorbed by chlorophyll for photosynthesis, and in the near infrared, reflected by the cellular structure of the leaves.   

The NDVI can be calculated using the formula: 
\begin{equation}
    NDVI= (NIR-RED)/(NIR+RED)
    \label{sum}
\end{equation} 
 
Where: 
\begin{itemize}
    \item NIR is reflectance in the near infrared (Band 8 in Sentinel-2);
    \item RED is the reflectance in the visible red (Band 4 in Sentinel-2).
\end{itemize}
 
NDVI values range from –1 to +1, indicating the absence of vegetation and dense, lush vegetation, respectively.
Studies conducted by \hyperref[bib:Piragnolo2021]{Piragnolo et al. (2021)} on the Vaia event confirm that NDVI is one of the most significant predictive variables for identifying damaged areas, recording a sudden drop in values immediately after the event. 

Although NDVI is a robust and effective indicator of forest cover, it has ecological limitations due to the difficulty of distinguishing between forest structure recovery and the growth of non-target vegetation \cite{Giannetti2021}.  

In post-Vaia areas, as documented by \hyperref[bib:Giupponi2023]{Giupponi et al. (2023)}, there is often a phenomenon of early greening, i.e. the process of rapid growth of pioneer herbaceous and shrub species that generates high NDVI values in the first few years after the storm, saturating the satellite signal. This phenomenon can lead to misinterpretations, suggesting, for example, that a dense bramble prairie represents a recovered forest.   

This raises the question of how to interpret NDVI time series using statistical approaches that manage uncertainty, such as the fuzzy logic proposed in this study, which allows recovery to be interpreted as a continuous gradient of ecological reorganisation. 

