\subsection{Satellite data}
To analyse forest continuity dynamics, images from the Copernicus Sentinel-2 L2A mission were utilized, acquired through the Copernicus Browser portal. The choice of this sensor is justified by three main factors: temporal resolution, spatial resolution and radiometric resolution \cite{ESA2021}. The monitoring frequency is 5 days, which is useful for providing cloud-free images in mountainous areas where the weather changes constantly and cloud cover causes problems with data acquisition at certain times. Sentinel-2 has variable spatial resolution, with a detail of 10 metres for the visible (B4) and near-infrared (B8) bands, and high radiometric resolution provides good sensitivity to variations in reflected light.   

All the images used were taken between September and mid-October (see Table 2 for details) to ensure data comparability and avoid variations in spectral parameters related to vegetation phenology. This prevents differences in NDVI values due to seasonality, allowing differences to be attributed to post-disturbance recovery dynamics. 
