\subsection{Theoretical approach: from Boolean logic to Fuzzy logic }
The study of land cover is generally based on Boolean logic, in which pixels are assigned a categorical membership (e.g., 1 for “forest,” 0 for “non-forest”). However, following disturbances such as Vaia, natural ecosystems present complex ecological gradients and transition zones that cannot be accurately described by binary classifications \citep{Rocchini2010, Giupponi2023}.   
For this reason, the model developed in this study uses Fuzzy logic. This approach allows a degree of membership $\mu$, continuous between 0 and 1, to be assigned to each pixel.  

\begin{itemize}
    \item Value $\mu$ = 1: represents the “ideal forest,” with high photosynthetic biomass and a closed canopy. 
    \item Value $\mu$ = 0: represents the total absence of forest characteristics (bare soil, rock, anthropised surfaces). 
    \item Intermediate values (0.2-0.7): describe the heterogeneity typical of post-disturbance environments, characterised by the coexistence of fallen logs, pioneer herbaceous vegetation, and shrubs.  
\end{itemize}
The main objective of this analysis is to monitor the trajectory of forest recovery over time. In this context, the identification of specific types of non-forest land cover, such as the distinction between bare soil and fallen dry logs, is secondary to the main objective. Instead, the focus is entirely on quantifying how close each pixel is to returning to a functional forest state. Using a continuous scale from 0 (non-forest) to 1 (full forest) ensures that the resulting temporal visualisations, such as Ridgeline graphs, remain immediately intuitive: a shift to the right of the curve clearly signals forest regrowth. Conversely, attempting to plot three or four distinct classes simultaneously would complicate interpretation. 

\subsection{Data acquisition and pre-processing (Sentinel-2)}
The study uses satellite images acquired by the Sentinel-2 mission. All images were taken between September and mid-October to ensure data comparability and avoid variations in spectral parameters related to phenology. This prevents differences in NDVI values due to seasonality, allowing for the attribution of differences to post-disturbance recovery dynamics. 

To ensure greater uniformity, a fixed area of interest of approximately 500m x 500m was defined. This ensures that the temporal comparison between 2017, 2019, 2021, 2023, and 2025 takes place on the same spatial grid. 

Furthermore, in order to avoid saturation and colour distortion, the NDVI vegetation index was calculated directly from the raw Sentinel-2 L2A bands: band 4 (red) and band 8 (NIR).   

To avoid the colour distortions or saturation typical of pre-processed products (e.g., 8-bit RGB images), the NDVI vegetation index was calculated directly from the raw Sentinel-2 L2A Bottom of Atmosphere (BOA) bands: Band 4 (red) and Band 8 (NIR). In this sense, the choice to use BOA images rather than Top of Atmosphere (TOA) images produced by Sentinel-2 L1C stems from the atmospheric correction to which the latter are subjected. In fact, the Sen2Cor algorithm analyses L1C images to create a “mask” that automatically distinguishes clouds, cloud shadows, snow, water, and vegetation. The algorithm then estimates how much water vapour and aerosols are in the air and subtracts them from the signal to obtain pure colours \cite{Main-Knorn2017}.

The formula applied is:
\begin{equation}
    NDVI = (B8 - B4)/(B8 + B4)
    \label{sum}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Statistical analysis and visualisation (Ridgeline Plots) }

All statistical analyses and data visualisations were performed using the R programming language. The data processing workflow was divided into three main operational steps: data extraction, visualisation, and statistical summarisation.

First, to analyse the temporal dynamics of each specific site, a precise Region of Interest (ROI) was defined for all six study areas. For each ROI, the fuzzy membership pixel values were extracted across the five reference years (2017, 2019, 2021, 2023, and 2025). This extraction process resulted in a continuous and structured temporal dataset containing the pixel distribution for each specific site before and after the Vaia storm.

Second, to visualise the temporal evolution of the forest recovery, Ridgeline plots were generated using the ggplot2 and ggridges packages in R. Instead of relying solely on single numerical averages, this specific type of chart was employed to display the probability density distribution of all the extracted pixel values for each year. This allowed for a direct visual assessment of how the shape, peak, and spread of the pixel distribution changed over time, effectively showing the landscape's fragmentation and subsequent reorganisation.

Finally, alongside the visual representation, quantitative summary statistics were calculated and organised into a tabular format for each area. For every analysed year, two main metrics were computed:

\begin{itemize}
    \item the NDVI Mean, which provides a quantification of photosynthetic vigour in the area, while its standard deviation measures its spatial heterogeneity. 
    \item The Fuzzy Mean and its standard deviation, representing the average recovery state of the pixels within the ROI and the spatial heterogeneity of the area.
    \item Delta ($\Delta$) metric, to standardise the evaluation of the recovery rate across different sites.
\end{itemize}

In particular, the last value was calculated as the difference between the Fuzzy Mean of any given year and the NDVI Mean of 2019 (the year of maximum damage), establishing a clear baseline to track the progressive spectral recovery. 

\section{Results}
This chapter presents the findings of the spatio-temporal analysis conducted on the six selected study areas, covering the period from the pre-disturbance baseline (2017) to the most recent images (2025). The evaluation of post-Vaia recovery dynamics is based on the integration of quantitative spectral metrics and statistical visualizations.

For each ROI, the analysis focuses on two complementary aspects: the recovery rate and the landscape heterogeneity. The temporal evolution of the Fuzzy mean and NDVI mean is reported to quantify the restoration of photosynthetic activity. To facilitate comparison across different sites, a Delta ($\Delta$) value is calculated relative to the year of maximum damage (2019), providing a direct measure of the speed of spectral recovery.

Instead, the structural reorganisation of the ecosystem is examined using Ridgeline Plots. These density distributions, combined with standard deviation, visualise the transition from the pre-storm homogeneous forest to the post-disturbance fragmented mosaic.

